//! Entries slice — Batch 215 (Plan 215) — the `TensorFold` native-engine
//! delta pack (Research 251), slot 1 part 3 (the coordinator-authored
//! member): the HIP 4-bit affine kernel's contraction-off exactness
//! contract. Source `ashhart/TensorFold` @ pin
//! `f1aef9c3f2ab85b2a68f2eed817e45eecbd04781` (origin/main @ 2026-10-10,
//! **Apache-2.0** — the 0.6.0+ license; this delta is post-flip). The two
//! source files were read IN FULL at the pin (this was the lane-B unread
//! pair the grading flagged): `zig/kernels/hip/affine.hip` (74 lines) +
//! `zig/src/hip/affine.zig` (122 lines). Quotes verified VERBATIM at the
//! pin via `git show f1aef9c3:<path>` (the working-tree checkout is
//! unusable on this box — an invalid filename in the tree — so every read
//! went through the blob path; line numbers below are `cat -n` positions
//! in the pin blobs).
//!
//! DEDUP (run at authoring, both corpora):
//! - `-ffp-contract / contraction / FMA contract` — zero owners: the
//!   exactness family owns dispatch-boundary emulation
//!   (`bit-exact-dispatch-fusion-boundary-emulation`) and sampling-probe
//!   refusals (`construction-identity-row-classes-replace-probe`), never
//!   COMPILATION-FLAG contraction control. The contraction-off atom is
//!   new on its axis.
//! - `row.?invarian|M.?independent|any M` — zero owners for the
//!   M-independence axis (the nearest hits are
//!   `shape-only-plan-row-invariant-kernel` B188 — plan-from-shape so a
//!   row's BITS never depend on the row count, the DISPATCH-admission
//!   face; this rule is the ARITHMETIC face — same law family, different
//!   mechanism, named in-rule).
//! - `single.?bit|mutation|signed zero|NaN payload` — the comparator
//!   discipline has one neighbor, `half-domain-exact-band-dequant-with-
//!   raw-bit-oracle` (raw-bit oracle for a dequant band); that owns the
//!   ORACLE shape for dequant tables, not the build-contract + mutation-
//!   sweep pair. Distinct, named in-rule.
//! - `RNE|round.?nearest.?even|bf16 convert` — zero rule owners (the
//!   conversion-chunking rule B206 is a bandwidth/staging rule).
//!
//! Category N (dtype + alignment specialization): the rule governs a
//! quantized dtype kernel's bit-exactness contract (4-bit packed words,
//! BF16 scales/biases/activations, BF16 output). The I-category
//! suggestion in the staging note does not fit — I is NSA block-sparse.
//! `NotBenchable` (the B136 decision-content class: the evidence is the
//! mutation-sweep comparator + the M-independence argument, never a
//! timing figure).

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::KernelRule;

pub(super) fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── N: dtype exactness (#463) — the quantized kernel that must
        // bit-match its reference for EVERY row count builds with
        // contraction off, reduces in one fixed source order, converts
        // once through explicit RNE, and is verified by a comparator
        // that rejects any single-bit mutation.
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "hip-affine-contract-off-row-invariance".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a quantized kernel that must bit-match a reference for EVERY row count pins its arithmetic with a BUILD CONTRACT, not a tolerance; category N dtype exactness): the 4-bit affine row-projection kernel is 'built with -ffp-contract=off so every row reduces the same way for any M' (the file header, verbatim) — 'Four-bit BF16 row projection, FP32 sums, explicit BF16 RNE output'. THE LAW: whether a*b+c contracts into an FMA is a COMPILER CHOICE made per specialization, and the choice can differ between specializations of the same kernel — different M reaches different unroll/occupancy decisions, a contracted arm rounds differently from an uncontracted arm, and bit-parity with the reference then holds only for the row counts that happened to be checked. Contraction OFF moves the arithmetic order out of the compiler's discretion and into the SOURCE TEXT, where it is the same for every M; the reference then matches by construction instead of by sampling. The flag is one half of the contract — it composes with four source-level halves that make the order fully pinned: (1) FIXED REDUCTION ORDER — each lane consumes '16 inputs per lane, four-product chains, 32-lane reduction' with the chain spelled as one tree, 'dot = dot + (((product[0] + product[1]) + product[2]) + product[3])', the warp reduce a fixed shuffle ladder ('for offset = 16; offset != 0; offset /= 2'), and ONE writer lane converts; (2) FP32 ACCUMULATION END-TO-END — the dot, the sum, and the per-group fold ('acc = acc + (from_bf16(scales[g]) * dot + sum * from_bf16(biases[g]))') all stay f32; the only narrowing is the final store; (3) ONE EXPLICIT RNE CONVERSION AT THE OUTPUT with NaN preservation — 'Preserve NaNs rather than rounding a small NaN payload to infinity' (the NaN branch shifts and sets the quiet bit instead of running the round-to-odd increment, which would carry a small payload into infinity); (4) A COMPARATOR STRICTER THAN TOLERANCE — 'Exact BF16 comparison also rejects infinities/NaNs on either side', exercised by the test named 'every single-bit output mutation is rejected including signed zero': the sweep flips EVERY bit of the golden output and demands the mismatch counter report exactly 1, so signed zero, a NaN payload bit, or a last-place mantissa flip each fail the gate — a tolerance-based compare cannot see any of them. ADMISSION GATES ride the same contract rather than specializing around it: the kernel returns unless 'bits != 4 || (group != 32 && group != 64)' is false, and the host Shape validator refuses bits != 4, group outside {32, 64}, and inputs not a multiple of the group — the exactness claim is scoped to the shapes the contract was validated for, and everything else declines loudly. LAUNCH GEOMETRY stays M-independent too: 'Two 32-lane output warps per block, independent of batch width' — grid (outputs+1)/2 x rows, so no row count changes a per-output reduction. RUST SPELLING (the hazard inverts): Rust never contracts a*b+c implicitly — there the same law bites through EXPLICIT mul_add calls (contraction ON where the reference had it OFF) and through platform intrinsic choices; the repair is the same four source halves (fixed order, wide accumulation, one explicit conversion, bit-exact comparator) with the flag half replaced by 'no mul_add below the parity boundary unless the reference specifies it'. REGIME: quantized/GPU kernels that must bit-match a reference — oracle fixtures, cross-backend parity gates, deterministic-replay serving; kernels whose exactness claim is quoted at 'any M' / 'any batch width'. ANTI-REGIME: paths where tolerance agreement suffices (never claim bit-parity for them); builds where the REFERENCE IS DEFINED AS the contracted output (then contract on BOTH sides and say so — the contract is symmetry, not the flag); inner-product hot paths where FMA contraction is load-bearing for speed AND no cross-M parity claim exists (measure before paying the flag — contraction-off can cost; this kernel buys exactness for a verification path, priced knowingly). NotBenchable (the B136 decision-content class — the evidence class is the mutation-sweep comparator and the M-independence argument, never a timing figure). KINSHIP (do not double-count): shape-only-plan-row-invariant-kernel (B188) is the SAME LAW's dispatch face — a plan derived from the shape alone so a row's bits never depend on the row count; THIS rule is the arithmetic face (the order the bits are computed in), and the two compose: shape-only admission chooses the kernel, contraction-off + fixed order keep every kernel instance bit-identical across M; construction-identity-row-classes-replace-probe (B199/N) owns the sampling-refusal discipline — a sampled-output probe cannot certify bit-identity — and this rule's mutation-sweep comparator is a verifying instrument one grade stricter than any sample; half-domain-exact-band-dequant-with-raw-bit-oracle owns the raw-bit ORACLE shape for a dequant band — the oracle ASKS 'are these the exact bits', while this rule pins HOW the bits get computed (flag + order + one conversion) and how strictly they are compared (any-single-bit mutation rejects); bit-exact-dispatch-fusion-boundary-emulation owns boundary emulation at the dispatch/fusion seam — compilation-flag contraction control was UNOWNED on that axis. CITE: ashhart/TensorFold @ f1aef9c3f2ab85b2a68f2eed817e45eecbd04781 (origin/main 2026-10-10; the work rides PR #463, Apache-2.0) — zig/kernels/hip/affine.hip (the header contract line 1, the kernel affine_row4_f32 lines 46-74, the to_bf16 NaN branch lines 30-36) + zig/src/hip/affine.zig (mismatchCount lines 5-14, the mutation-sweep test lines 16-26, Shape::validate lines 36-45, the launch comment line 63). Batch 215 / Plan 215.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "#![allow(dead_code)]\n// naive: contraction decided per-specialization plus a tolerance comparator.\n// The batched rows go through the contracted arm (mul_add: one rounding over\n// the exact product), the remainder rows through the uncontracted arm\n// (separate mul then add: the product rounds, then the add rounds) -- same\n// inputs, different bits, and the tolerance compare cannot see it.\n\nfn row_output_naive(x: f32, w: f32, b: f32, batched: bool) -> f32 {\n    if batched {\n        x.mul_add(w, b) // contracted: one rounding\n    } else {\n        x * w + b // uncontracted: two roundings\n    }\n}\n\nfn matches_within_tolerance(a: f32, b: f32) -> bool {\n    (a - b).abs() < 1e-2 // blind to 1-ULP and payload drift\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn naive_arms_drift_and_tolerance_is_blind() {\n    // x = w = 1 + 2^-12: the exact product is 1 + 2^-11 + 2^-24. One ulp at\n    // [1, 2) is 2^-23, so the tail term is EXACTLY half an ulp: the product\n    // rounds to even, down to 1 + 2^-11. b = 2^-24 is half an ulp too.\n    // contracted: exact product + b = 1 + 2^-11 + 2^-23 -- exactly\n    // representable, so the single rounding KEEPS the tail.\n    // uncontracted: round(product) + b lands on the same half-ulp tie and\n    // rounds back down to 1 + 2^-11 -- one ulp below the contracted arm.\n    let two = 2.0f32;\n    let x = 1.0f32 + two.powi(-12);\n    let w = x;\n    let b = two.powi(-24);\n    let batched = row_output_naive(x, w, b, true);\n    let remainder = row_output_naive(x, w, b, false);\n    // the drift the whole rule exists to catch -- exactly one ulp:\n    assert_ne!(batched.to_bits(), remainder.to_bits());\n    assert_eq!(batched, 1.0 + two.powi(-11) + two.powi(-23));\n    assert_eq!(remainder, 1.0 + two.powi(-11));\n    // ...and the naive gate waves it through:\n    assert!(matches_within_tolerance(batched, remainder));\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "#![allow(dead_code)]\n// repaired: the four source halves, the flag half spelled as the no-mul_add\n// parity boundary. The gate: bf16 output patterns must be BIT-identical\n// across every arm (any M reduces alike), and the comparator rejects any\n// single-bit mutation -- signed zero, NaN payloads, infinities on either\n// side -- where a tolerance compare waves them all through.\n\nfn row_output(x: f32, w: f32, b: f32, batched: bool) -> f32 {\n    // fixed source order, no mul_add below the parity boundary: one rounding\n    // sequence for every arm, so any M reduces alike\n    let _ = batched;\n    let p = x * w;\n    p + b\n}\n\n// the drift twin (the ref arm's defect, modeled locally so this arm is\n// self-contained): contraction ON where the reference had it OFF.\nfn row_output_contracted(x: f32, w: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    x.mul_add(w, b)\n}\n\n// explicit RNE narrowing with NaN preservation (the to_bf16 shape).\nfn rne_bf16(x: f32) -> u16 {\n    let u = x.to_bits();\n    if u & 0x7fff_ffff > 0x7f80_0000 {\n        // Preserve NaNs rather than rounding a small payload to infinity\n        return ((u >> 16) | 0x40) as u16;\n    }\n    ((u + 0x7fff + ((u >> 16) & 1)) >> 16) as u16\n}\n\n// Exact comparison also rejects infinities/NaNs on either side.\nfn mismatch_count(actual: &[u16], expected: &[u16]) -> usize {\n    assert_eq!(actual.len(), expected.len());\n    let mut n = 0usize;\n    for i in 0..actual.len() {\n        let a = actual[i];\n        let e = expected[i];\n        if a != e || (a & 0x7f80) == 0x7f80 || (e & 0x7f80) == 0x7f80 {\n            n += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    n\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_arm_reduces_identically_and_mutation_sweep_rejects() {\n    let two = 2.0f32;\n    // the same inputs the ref arm drifts on (x = w = 1 + 2^-12, b = 2^-24):\n    // the contraction drift the contract forbids is REAL on these inputs --\n    // and the repaired form's two arms cannot produce it.\n    let x = 1.0f32 + two.powi(-12);\n    let w = x;\n    let b = two.powi(-24);\n    assert_ne!(\n        row_output_contracted(x, w, b).to_bits(),\n        (x * w + b).to_bits(),\n        \"the contraction drift must be real on the model inputs\"\n    );\n    assert_eq!(\n        row_output(x, w, b, true).to_bits(),\n        row_output(x, w, b, false).to_bits(),\n        \"the M-independence property: the flag cannot change the bits\"\n    );\n\n    // the golden: FINITE bf16 patterns only -- the comparator rejects NaN\n    // and infinities on EITHER side, so a golden cannot contain them.\n    let golden = [rne_bf16(0.773_437_5f32), rne_bf16(-2.414_062_5f32)];\n    assert_eq!(mismatch_count(&golden, &golden), 0);\n    // the mutation sweep: flip every bit of golden[0]; each flip counts\n    // exactly one mismatch -- signed zero included.\n    for i in 0..16 {\n        let mut changed = golden;\n        changed[0] ^= 1u16 << i;\n        assert_eq!(mismatch_count(&changed, &golden), 1, \"bit flip unseen\");\n    }\n    // signed zero is a real difference:\n    assert_eq!(mismatch_count(&[0x8000], &[0x0000]), 1);\n    // ...and so are bit-identical NaN and infinity pairs -- rejected on\n    // either side even when every bit agrees:\n    assert_eq!(mismatch_count(&[0x7fc0], &[0x7fc0]), 1); // NaN\n    assert_eq!(mismatch_count(&[0x7f80], &[0x7f80]), 1); // +inf\n\n    // the NaN branch preserved a payload instead of rounding to infinity:\n    let nan_kept = rne_bf16(f32::NAN);\n    assert_eq!(nan_kept & 0x7f80, 0x7f80); // still NaN-class\n    assert_eq!(nan_kept & 0x40, 0x40); // quietened, payload kept\n\n    // the admission gates decline out-of-contract shapes loudly:\n    fn shape_ok(bits: u8, group: usize, inputs: usize) -> bool {\n        bits == 4 && (group == 32 || group == 64) && inputs % group == 0\n    }\n    assert!(shape_ok(4, 32, 96));\n    assert!(!shape_ok(8, 32, 96));\n    assert!(!shape_ok(4, 16, 96));\n    assert!(!shape_ok(4, 32, 95));\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
